#edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "by substituting $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310311} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbe8906-11f6-5f0e-8be5-7e60e92604ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using the fact that ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2f320d0-2a11-5928-b1f8-8a5d700416e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "by substituting $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310311} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e482e3-780a-5e91-b090-efa6a61b43ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "470e1341-576f-51bf-8372-83789f8096ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of matrix exponential" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309291} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc59270-51b2-5c62-b938-b48c9f3532b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310038} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fb24cf-f960-5624-9b68-8fb29c368dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310437} {:answer/id #uuid "e47a9c9c-d36b-555e-b8a9-70fb22918198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e47a9c9c-d36b-555e-b8a9-70fb22918198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310572} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f5da27-dc12-5c0e-8352-c31ac3b14b77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da9645d1-ab1f-59b5-93f4-27c23f00e120" :question/math-academy-id "q-336639" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ is true by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution of the system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ is true by $\\text{the properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Starting from $\\text{L3},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe evaluate both sides at $t={t}_{0}$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ $\\text{by substituting }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$"}]}
